[{:seon.task/name "source-at-the-repl"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: in an agent, `clojure.repl/source` and `source-fn` answer a var's recorded text (interned into clojure.repl from my.agent at the agent's start, as clojure.main's REPL refers them), reading the program facts (:form/src, `seon.docs/source-of`) since program code lives as facts, not files. Reproduction it answers: agents evaluated (clojure.repl/source f) or (clojure.repl/source-fn 'ns/f) 171 times in one run (\"No such var: clojure.repl/source\"), and slurped a resource path 53 times (\"Cannot open <nil> as a Reader\"). Owed: its proof, for a program (not platform) definition. Estimate: one AVET seek per call (`seon.db/eid`'s cost)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/source-answers-a-program-definition-from-its-facts}}
 {:seon.task/name "eval-record-keeps-a-failing-form-whole"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an eval record's :form is the form printed under the :form budget (600 tokens), so a failing form is cut (…N), and a defn that failed could only be recovered from the model's reply. Correct: for a form that errs, record the exact text the agent sent (cut from the reader's positions, as :var/src is for defs), bounded only by the request itself; a form that succeeds keeps its printed echo. Cost follows the failing forms only."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-failing-form-is-recorded-whole}}
 {:seon.task/name "source-reads-the-program"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `(clojure.repl/source seon.reconcile/root-causes)` and `clojure.repl/source-fn` answer, in an agent's REPL, a platform or program var's recorded form text (:form/src, imported by `seon.source`) from the agent's branch db, its own redefinition if it made one, the way `doc` does, through the one reader `seon.docs/source-of`. Before, a compiled platform var had no source file the agent could read, and the first DeepSeek agent on Seon's own code spent 8 turns exploring around the error."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-agent-repl-answers-what-a-clojure-repl-answers}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-eval-error-never-ends-the-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: run seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took#1 ended :closed :error at turn 0, reason \"seon.turn/work! threw: No matching field found: exists for class java.lang.String\", error {:seon.error/type :seon.agent/eval-error :seon.error/operation seon.repl/eval-code :class IllegalArgumentException}; its branch shows every form of `my.root` re-evaluated unchanged (a reload or load-file). A plain (load-file \"src/my/root.clj\") or (require 'my.root :reload) in a fresh agent answers nil (not reproduced). Cause: an evaluation error escaped `seon.repl/eval-code` into `seon.turn/work!`, and the turn was not recorded, so the form that raised it is lost. Correct: find the form and path that raise it (reload my.root after the agent's other turn-0 forms; a load whose form calls a File method on a path string); every error raised while a loaded file's forms evaluate reaches the agent as that load's :err item (as Clojure's require throws and the REPL prints it), never the end of its run; record the turn before anything after the evaluation can fail."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-error-inside-a-loaded-file-is-the-loads-error-item}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-item-shape-for-an-eval"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.repl/record-tx` stores a reply item's :printed as :shown and :err as :error, so ``seon.render.ui/eval-out`` reads (or :printed :shown). Storing the item's own keys saves about 3 lines, but stored records would still need :shown read beside :printed: a permanent dual reader, the fallback AGENTS §9 names. Not worth doing as a rename alone; worth doing only if the stored :seon.eval/record values are rewritten too (the standing store is test data, erased: DECISIONS: The standing store is test data), so one reader remains."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-read-eval-loop-is-clojure-mains"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.repl/eval-code` (repl.clj:55-123) is its own read-eval loop: a LineNumberingPushbackReader per request, *1 *2 *3 *e bound by hand (:89-91), ns tracked per form. Jolt ships clojure.main/repl with :read, :eval, :print and :caught hooks (reference-code/jolt/stdlib/clojure/main.clj:431). Correct: eval-code is clojure.main/repl with hooks: :read keeps each form's exact text and position and returns request-exit on an interrupt; :eval is the request's eval-fn under seon.intr/run with its ticks; :caught the one error map. Prove at the REPL first that an interrupt during :read and a per-form output capture behave; stop and report if not."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-agent-repl-answers-what-a-clojure-repl-answers seon.repl-test/an-interrupt-stops-only-the-request-it-arrives-in}}
 {:seon.task/name "type-findings-are-an-evals-warnings"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: Jolt checks the success types of calls (`jolt.passes.types/check-form`, a public function returning [{:op :argpos :type :msg :pos}] for provably wrong calls, non-strict by default), but only prints them to stderr when the environment variable JOLT_CHECK is set (host/chez/compile-eval.ss); stderr reaches an agent only after its death, so an eval never shows them. Our REPL already holds each analyzed node: `seon.repl/wrap-record` binds `jolt.analyzer/*unit-tap*` to `seon.program/tap`, which is called with [form node], and a reply item has a `:warnings` key (`seon.repl/wrap-instrument` fills it with `:uncontracted-definition`). Correct: a small step of the same wrap calls `check-form` on each node the tap holds and adds its findings to `:warnings` as data (no environment variable, nothing refused: it records, AGENTS §4), rendered with the item. Measured silent on all of `src` (3,397 forms, 0 findings: docs/errors.md#an-eval-errors-diagnostics); not measured yet: the cost per form, owed as a cost proof of work done. Gate: the owner's go, since it adds content to an eval item (AGENTS §3 root's gate, 4). Deletes nothing; it replaces the idea of setting JOLT_CHECK. Jolt's JOLT_DIAG (the CLI's uncaught-error report), JOLT_DEBUG (build and cache diagnostics) and its nREPL server are deliberately unused: docs/errors.md#an-eval-errors-diagnostics."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-provably-wrong-call-is-a-warning-on-its-item seon.repl-test/a-checked-form-costs-its-nodes seon.repl-test/our-own-source-loads-with-no-type-warnings}}
 {:seon.task/name "err-text-is-the-err-stream"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an eval item's :err-text is `(str err (nrepl/err->msg v {:outer outer}))` (`seon.repl/eval-code`, two more producers at the read error and the entrant boundary), so it holds two things under one key: what the form wrote to `*err*`, and a second error render (Jolt's text, its position request-relative). The error's own render is :printed (`seon.render.ai/error`) and the error map's data carries the position in the form's terms (:seon.error/at). Only `repl_test` reads :err-text (`a-response-carries-values-not-their-printed-text` asserts it includes the divide-by-zero message). Correct: the `*err*` stream is its own item key beside :out (`:stderr`, present when the form wrote anything), the err->msg text and the `outer-frames` capture that feeds only it are deleted, and the test asserts the error's :printed. Deletes: :err-text from the item keys (`seon.repl` item-keys), the three producers, `outer-frames`. Replaces a second render by the one."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-response-carries-values-not-their-printed-text seon.repl-test/a-form-that-writes-to-err-answers-it-as-stderr}}
 {:seon.task/name "records-keep-the-exact-value"
  :seon.task/plan "Storage keeps the exact value; renders budget it (AGENTS.md section 9: a clipping function). Left in src: `seon.proofs/failures` keeps the first `seon.proofs/max-assertions` failing assertions per test and drops the rest with no count; seon.root records a dead agent's stderr as its last tail-bytes (2048) though the file (`seon.agent/stderr-path`) is kept beside the store. Gone already: `seon.agent/error-map`'s message-chars cut, seon.model's 300-char HTTP error body, seon.learn's 80-char form and seon.agent-proc's 200-char form in :last-op and :running. Correct: each record holds the whole value or a reference to it (the stderr file's path and size; all assertions, or the first n plus :omitted n and the run's handle); the context and the dashboard render them under their budgets through the one printer. Deletes max-assertions and tail-bytes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.repl/eval-record-keeps-a-failing-form-whole"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-exception-message-is-recorded-whole
                      seon.proofs-test/every-failing-assertion-is-kept-or-counted
                      seon.problems-test/a-death-record-points-at-the-whole-stderr}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-record-stores-its-value-within-a-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "From the branch review of jolt/agent-conversation (2026-10-25, root's scratchpad branch-review.md, follow-ups): `seon.repl/record-details` stores any plain value whole, on main too for root's outside and UI evals, with an O(value) plain? walk and no bound: storage dedupes by content key, but main grows with every large value. Correct: state the bound (a config key, the value's size by the store's own measure) past which a value is stored as its printed form and type, as a non-plain one is, saying so; or measure that none is needed. Proof: a record past the bound holds :printed and :type and says why; one under it holds the value."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "an-eval-answer-carries-its-warnings"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the independent review of the one derivation's revision 4 (notes/archive/one-derivation-design-rev4-review.md, 2026-10-26): seon.repl/instrument computes, for each form that defines a function, its contract armed or an uncontracted-definition warning naming every uncontracted definition so far with its fix, and seon.repl/response built the eval's one answer without them, so the agent never read the warning a merge later refuses on. The answer carries :warnings: the newest form's uncontracted-definition warning (it names every one so far) and each contract that could not be armed (:invalid-contract with its error). Root's ruling, 2026-10-26: a correctness bug, first after slice 2's merge."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-uncontracted-definition-is-told-on-its-answer}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-runs-in-one-mode"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the guard audit of 2026-10-25 (notes/archive/guard-audit-2026-10-25.md, S3-S6; checked against main 2026-10-27) and the blocking-calls audit (row 2). What exists: seon.repl/run! picks jolt.host/main-carrier-run! only when (lib/macos?) (repl.clj:573), seon.intr registers signal wakes only when (jolt.host/main-carrier?) (intr.clj:80, 92), and seon.sock/recv! chooses a parking read or the blocking recvmsg arm by (and main-carrier? fiber?) (sock.clj:195). The agent therefore forks safely and parks on macOS and neither elsewhere: each branch defends the other mode. Correct: the agent runs on the main carrier on every OS (needs jolt.io-poller/the-io-poller-wakes-on-signals-on-linux), recv! is always MSG_DONTWAIT plus poller/wait-ready, and seon.lib/macos? (lib.clj:219), the park? test and the blocking arm are deleted. Proof to write: an agent on Linux parks on root's socket and answers an interrupt."
  :seon.task/kind :work}]
